    "certificate": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "layout": "prose",
        "anchor_id": "masah",
        "eyebrow": "If you’re new to this",
        "heading": "What masah is",
        "prose": "<p>Masah means wiping over your socks during wudu instead of taking them off and washing your feet. It’s permitted on footwear that meets certain conditions, and scholars have written about those conditions for a very long time.</p><p>In everyday terms, it means you can make wudu at your desk, at the mosque, or at a service station, without finding somewhere to wash your feet and without going back to your prayer in wet socks.</p>",
        "footnote": "We make socks. We’re not scholars, and nothing on this page is a ruling. We’ll tell you what the socks are and what the conditions are, so you can decide — or ask someone qualified."
      }
    },
    "conditions": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "wash",
        "layout": "list",
        "numbered": true,
        "heading": "What the socks have to do",
        "eyebrow": "The conditions",
        "row_scale": "large",
        "media_fallback": "wudu-design-sheet.jpg",
        "media_alt": "HydroSox wudu design sheet: structured construction that holds its shape and stands upright when not worn, durable waterproof materials, and long-term durability.",
        "media_caption": "A pair of black HydroSox standing upright unworn, holding their shape.",
        "lede": "<p>Scholars set conditions for the footwear you’re allowed to wipe over. Three of them are about the sock itself, and those are the three we can speak to.</p>",
        "footnote": "There’s one more condition, and it’s about you rather than the socks. You put them on after a full wudu, with your feet washed. From your next wudu onwards, you wipe."
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Water mustn’t get through to your foot",
            "body": "<p>Cotton socks soak through. Ours have a Porelle® waterproof layer sealed inside them, so water doesn’t reach the skin. It’s the same layer in every pair we make — there’s no separate wudu version.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "They have to hold their shape",
            "body": "<p>They need to sit over the foot as a covering rather than collapse flat against it. Ours stand up on their own when you take them off, and there’s a photograph of that below.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "They have to stay on your foot",
            "body": "<p>Shaped so they stay in place through a normal day rather than working loose and sliding down.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "i1",
        "i2",
        "i3"
      ]
    },
    "credentials": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "layout": "split",
        "numbered": false,
        "heading": "Who has looked at them",
        "head_note": "<p>The differentiator is the named membrane. Everything else in this category tends to be asserted rather than evidenced.</p>",
        "eyebrow": "Examined by",
        "lede": "<p>We didn’t want to make a religious claim about our own socks, so we asked people qualified to look at them.</p>",
        "wide_head": true,
        "footnote": "There’s no certificate, because no organisation issues certificates for socks. What we have is two qualified scholars who examined them. If you follow a different school or a different scholar, ask them. We’d rather you were sure than take our word for it.",
        "link_label": "",
        "link_url": ""
      },
      "blocks": {
        "sc1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "<a href=\"https://www.jkn.org.uk/about-us/\" rel=\"noopener\">Shaykh Mufti Saiful Islam</a>",
            "body": "<p>Founder and Principal of the JKN Institute in Bradford, where he also heads the fatwa service.</p>"
          }
        },
        "sc2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amjad_M._Mohammed\" rel=\"noopener\">Mufti Amjad Mohammed</a>",
            "body": "<p>Founder and Principal of Dār al-ʿUlūm al-Zaytuniyya in Bradford, and a lecturer in Islamic jurisprudence. He has written extensively on Islamic law for Muslims living in Britain.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "sc1",
        "sc2"
      ]
    },
    "travel": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "layout": "band",
        "numbered": false,
        "heading": "Hajj, Umrah and travel",
        "eyebrow": "Travelling"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "tv1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Long days, shared facilities",
            "body": "<p>Pilgrimage means long days, busy washrooms and nowhere to dry anything properly. A pair that keeps water out and dries overnight is worth the space in the bag.</p>"
          }
        },
        "tv2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Take more than one pair",
            "body": "<p>Most people travelling take two or three. They wash cool and air dry, which is slower than a tumble dryer, so one pair won’t keep up.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "tv1",
        "tv2"
      ]
