    "faq": {
      "type": "faq-accordion",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are wudu socks?",
            "answer": "<p>Waterproof socks made so you can wipe over them during wudu instead of washing your feet. They need to keep water out, hold their shape and stay on the foot — those are the conditions scholars set for the footwear itself.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are these certified for wudu?",
            "answer": "<p>No, and no sock is — there’s no organisation that issues certificates for this. What we have is two UK scholars who examined the socks.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I still have to wash my feet?",
            "answer": "<p>You wash them as part of a full wudu before the socks go on. From your next wudu onwards, you wipe over them instead. The masah page sets it out step by step.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How long can I keep wiping for?",
            "answer": "<p>Scholars give twenty-four hours if you’re at home and seventy-two if you’re travelling. The clock starts at your first wipe after wudu breaks, not when you put the socks on. That’s the part most people have slightly wrong.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What’s different about these and your other socks?",
            "answer": "<p>Nothing in the waterproof layer — it’s the same in every pair. What matters for masah is that the sock holds its shape rather than collapsing onto the foot, and that’s built into how it’s knitted.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are these the same as leather socks or khuffs?",
            "answer": "<p>Not the same material. Khuffs are the leather footwear the classical rulings were written about. These are a modern waterproof version aimed at the same three properties. Whether that counts as equivalent is a question for scholars, and the masah page explains how it’s discussed.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can women wear them?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. They’re unisex and start at UK 3. Go by your foot length rather than the men’s or women’s size you’re used to.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will washing them ruin them?",
            "answer": "<p>Cool wash, inside out, no fabric softener, then air dry away from the radiator. Heat and softener are what finish off a waterproof layer, not wearing them.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
